# AGENTS.md

Instructions for AI coding agents (and humans) working in SampleApp. SampleApp uses the VSA framework: Vertical Slice Architecture with one folder for each module.

## Layout

| Path             | Purpose                                                              |
|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `App/Host/`      | The web host. `Program.cs` calls `builder.AddVsa()`, which finds the modules |
| `App/Views/`     | The Blazor views. Each module has its page in `App/Views/Pages/`     |
| `Modules/<Name>/`| One module: its root project, `Projects/` (one project for each layer), `Domain/`, `Infrastructure/`, `Presenters/` and `Slices/<Feature>/` |
| `Shared/<Name>/` | One Shared module: a base of the same layers in each module. It has the layout of a module |
| `Tests/`         | The VSA architecture tests, and one unit test file for each module  |

A module project is only `<Project Sdk="VSA.Sdk" />`. VSA.Sdk gives each project its files and its references. In `Slices/`, the type in each file selects its project: requests and responses go to `Contracts.Requests`, events to `Contracts.Events`, and the other files to the handlers project of the slice. Do not add references or `Compile` items to a module project by hand: the build fails with `VSA0001` or `VSA0006`.

A Shared module is for base types of the app, as the VSA framework is the base of each layer: for example a message base in `Shared/Pipeline/Slices/Message/`. A Shared module has the same slice layout as a module. Each layer of each module gets the same layer of each Shared module. A Shared module never uses a module. A Shared layer that uses another Shared module names it in `<VsaUses>`, in its project file. Its root has one `Module` class. The host configures the Shared modules first, by name, and it loads them through the modules.

## Commands

Use the `vsa` tool to add code. It writes only new files, and it does not write over a file.

```bash
dotnet tool restore                          # one time, after a new clone
dotnet vsa add module <Name>                 # all layers, a status request and a page
dotnet vsa add request <Name> -m <Module>    # a request, its response and its handler
dotnet vsa doctor                            # the tool, VSA.Sdk and the packages have one version
dotnet test SampleApp.slnx                   # the architecture tests and the unit tests
```

The architecture tests fail until the app has a module: with no module, each rule has nothing to check.

The VSA packages come from GitHub Packages. Set `GITHUB_TOKEN` before a restore, for example to the output of `gh auth token`.

To update VSA, change the version in `global.json`, `dotnet-tools.json` and the `VSA.*` package references, then run `dotnet vsa doctor`.

## Core principles

- **Organize by feature, not by layer.** Each slice is one folder, `Slices/<Feature>/`, with its request, response, events and handler. A slice has one kind of handler.
- **Modules are independent.** A module uses another module only through its contracts.
- **Keep shared code small.** Share code only when two or more slices need it.
